{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HARNESS RUN 5: 48 PASSED / 1 FAILED — AND THE ONE FAILURE IS THE ORACLE FIX EARNING ITS KEEP. ===\n\nRun 30767546938, job id 91548842532, main @ d72c4777d3 — the first tree containing EVERYTHING:\nd067's status-transition fix (1220/1221), the assignee-oracle rewrite (1226/1227), and cc77\n(1222-1225). Canary: [jira-live-canary] collected=49 executed=49 skipped=0, so nothing was silently\nskipped. 1631.25s.\n\nGREEN, INCLUDING THE CELL THAT BLOCKED THIS STORY LONGEST:\n    PASSED test_outbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status]\nThat is d067's fix confirmed live for the second consecutive run, and d067 is now CLOSED.\n\nTHE SINGLE FAILURE IS NEW INFORMATION, NOT A REGRESSION. The assignee oracle's SETUP now SUCCEEDS —\nthe rewrite's two setup steps (unassign+sync+assert-cleared, then remove the one mapping and assert\nthe absence it just created) worked, so for the FIRST TIME the cell got past its precondition and\nreached its REAL assertion. That assertion then failed:\n\n    AssertionError: THE PASS MINTED NOTHING: jira/'admin' still resolves to no identity in the store\n    copy after an inbound pass that carried that assignee. This is bug 5f48's silent-swallow\n    signature — the mint is best-effort and swallows its own failure, so the registry is the only\n    place it is observable.\n\nREAD THAT CAREFULLY, BECAUSE IT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS STORY. The ORIGINAL oracle compared\nensure_identity_for's return against itself and could never fail — it reported this exact path as\nPASSING. The rewrite made it discriminating; the first time it could speak, it said the identity is\nnot being minted. Whether that is a product defect or an artifact of the new setup is NOT yet\nsettled and is under diagnosis — but either way the previous green was worthless, which is the\nconcrete vindication of sibling ticket 2944's thesis that a test which cannot fail is worse than no\ntest.\n\nWHAT IS BEING SETTLED NOW (do not pre-judge it): either\n (a) the inbound pass genuinely fails to mint on a live DC instance — which would be a regression of\n     [rebar:5f48-244c-7063-42d0] (closed earlier in this epic) or a case its fix never covered, and\n     would become its own bug parented to e369 under this epic's standing precedent; or\n (b) the new setup leaves the pass with nothing to act on — the update-path mint at\n     apply_inbound_records.py:369 only runs when 'assignee' in fields, and re-assigning the SAME\n     user leaves the inbound differ nothing to report (inbound_fields.py:102-128), so an\n     unassign-then-nothing sequence could ask for a mint that was never going to happen.\nThe discriminator is that the oracle was proven able to go GREEN for the right reason locally,\ndriven through the production _ensure_inbound_assignee_identity with a DC-shaped user object. So\nsomething differs between that drive and the live pass, and naming it is the task.\n\nTHIS STORY CANNOT CLOSE UNTIL THAT CELL IS GREEN. e369 is otherwise 30/31 with only this story open."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785708359770403001,"uuid":"88c67722-6c1b-40a2-8aad-24f8ce1c4a7e"}